{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Toulouse and a claimant to the throne of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was born in 1150 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures in 1165 and they had several children together. He also had a relationship with Melisende of Arsuf and had a daughter with her.\n\n* **Succession and rule** *\nRaymond III became the Count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his father. He claimed the throne of the Kingdom of Jerusalem but was not successful in his claim. He was however a key ally to the Kingdom of Jerusalem and fought in several battles against the Ayyubid dynasty. He was also a member of the Third Crusade.\n\nRaymond III was involved in a number of military campaigns including the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 and the Battle of Marj Ayyun in 1179. He was also involved in the siege of Darbaid in 1180.\n\n* **Relationship with Saladin** *\nRaymond III had a complex relationship with Saladin who was the leader of the Ayyubid dynasty. While they were often at odds with each other they also had a number of agreements. In 1177 Raymond III and Saladin agreed to a treaty that would have ended the conflict between them but it was not ratified.\n\n* **Death and legacy** *\nRaymond III died in 1187 after being killed in battle. His death was a significant blow to the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli. He was succeeded by his son Bohemond IV Count of Tripoli who went on to play a key role in the Fifth Crusade.\n\nRaymond III's legacy is complex and often conflicting. He was a key ally to the Kingdom of Jerusalem but also had a number of disagreements with them. He was also a skilled military leader who fought in a number of battles against the Ayyubid dynasty.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0